.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_FULL_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_full_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map with custom allocation and comparison, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_full_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs,
                            custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name with the companion type
variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred macro,
then creates a map that takes its memory from mmgmt_procs and orders its
keys with custom_comparison_proc. The handle also gets the cleanup
attribute, so the map is destroyed on every exit from the enclosing scope,
an early return included.

It is cbmap_construct_full(3) with that cleanup attribute added, and the
widest of the scoped construct macros. cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the
same with the default allocator and the default order,
cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) passes only an allocator, and
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) only a comparison procedure. Either argument
can be NULL to select the matching default, which lets this one macro
cover every combination.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. Every later macro reads a key through
                        it, and the container takes from it the width to
                        expect and the alignment of a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never passes it to the container
mmgmt_procs             Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                        default allocator
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro declares variables and doesn't expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure receives pointers to two keys and returns a negative value
when the first key orders before the second, a positive value when it
orders after the second, and zero when the map must treat the two as the
same key. Because a zero turns an insert into an update, the procedure
defines key equality as well as key order.

It must give a strict weak order over key_t that doesn't change while the
map holds elements. It gets two bare pointers and no lengths, so it can
only read a width that key_t fixes. cbmap_create_full(3) describes the
default orders that a procedure replaces.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation
fails. When the caller has to handle a failure instead, use
cbmap_declare_scoped(3) with cbmap_init_full(3). The cleanup attribute
means the macro needs GCC or Clang.

If the caller passes a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, none of its four members may
be NULL. The map copies the procedures into itself, but the procedures and
the comparison procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map,
which here ends at the closing brace of the enclosing scope.

A walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that the
map was given; cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract.

The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle, so an
explicit cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope is fine too.

The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there.

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. Keys and values
are copied by assignment and C can't assign an array, so the map would
otherwise keep the address of the caller's array instead of its elements.
A struct that holds the array works.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Don't return it or store it
anywhere that lives longer than the scope: the cleanup attribute frees the
map when the scope exits, and the stored handle would then name a freed
map.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

void summarize(version *v, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_full_scoped(releases, version, int, &procs, version_cmp);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(releases, v[i], one);
    }
}                             /* The macro destroys the releases map here */
.fi
